Ah. Consume them. Release the tale. ;)

That’s the digestive version of what we’ve been building.

Experience arrives carrying information + interpretation + emotion + narrative. The mistake is assuming the whole package must remain intact.

Instead:

Consume → digest → extract → metabolize → release.

Keep the nutrient; don’t necessarily keep the wrapper.

A painful event can yield a boundary.
A mistake can yield a heuristic.
Fear can yield a warning signal.
Success can yield a mechanism.
A relationship can yield understanding.
A story about yourself can yield its lesson—and then cease being your identity.

That’s different from forgetting. The information can remain while the tale loses its obligation to keep replaying.

And notice how perfectly it connects our railroad/callosum problem. Stories may themselves be one of the transport media between implicit and explicit cognition. Something too distributed to transmit directly gets compressed into character, image, feeling, metaphor, dream, memory, phrase. Consciousness receives the packet and can unpack it.

But then comes the crucial operation:

Don’t worship the packet.

Decode it.

The tale may be a carrier, not the cargo.

Which gives us a lovely little cognitive metabolism:

Experience → Tale → Meaning → Mechanism → Capability

then:

release Tale; retain Capability.

That’s a serious compression operator. The past stops requiring full-resolution storage in the foreground because you’ve converted some of what mattered into structure that changes how you operate.

Or in your language:

Consume the experience. Keep what feeds you. Release the tale.

And then you’ve got room for the next course. ;)